    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass \u2014 **42 tests, 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS**, including the three new `Cp29Tests` postcode cases and the existing suite.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added an optional, validated `postcode` field to the owner create flow. The change is backward-compatible: an owner created without a postcode is still accepted.\n\n**What I changed:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `postcode` to the `OwnerFields` schema (4-digit string, optional). This regenerates both `OwnerFieldsDto` (request) and `OwnerDto` (response) with the field, and MapStruct wires it through automatically in both directions.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `postcode` column + getter/setter. Reused the existing `getLocality()` (city\u2192region table: Sydney=NSW, Melbourne=VIC, Brisbane=QLD, else UNKNOWN) for validation.\n\n3. **DB schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `postcode` column so the stored value persists (tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`).\n\n4. **`ValidateOwnerPostcode.java`** \u2014 new pipeline function. When a postcode is present it must be exactly four digits and, for a known region, fall in range (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099). An UNKNOWN-region city accepts any 4-digit code; absent/blank is skipped. Otherwise it throws the checked `InvalidPostcodeException`.\n\n5. **`InvalidPostcodeException` + handler + escalation YAML** \u2014 following the existing `InvalidEmail` pattern, responds **400** with `{\"errors\":[\"postcode\"]}`.\n\n6. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `postcode` step right after `build` (so the built entity and its derived locality are available), before the rest of the pipeline.\n\nValidation is done explicitly in the function rather than relying on bean validation, so both malformed (`\"12\"` \u2192 400) and out-of-range postcodes are reliably rejected regardless of how `@RequestBody` binding is wired.",
